# Line endings are a correctness concern here, not a style preference.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: with no .gitattributes, the working-tree line endings of
# a clone are decided by each user's `core.autocrlf`. On Git for Windows that
# defaults to `true`, so a fresh clone rewrote every LF to CRLF — and the
# Workflow tool refuses a script containing control characters, which CR
# (U+000D) is. The result was that all three `.claude/workflows/*.js` scripts
# were rejected at launch on the most common platform, while the committed blobs
# were clean LF and every check passed. The repo looked correct and the
# capability was dead on arrival.
#
# `eol=lf` below is authoritative and overrides core.autocrlf, so the working
# tree matches the blob on every platform.
#
# EXISTING CLONES do not self-heal: git only rewrites files it checks out. After
# pulling this, run `git add --renormalize . && git checkout -- .` (or simply
# re-clone) if any working file still has CRLF. Guarded by deterministic check
# D30, which fails on a CR in any file the Workflow tool parses.

# Default: treat as text, detect binary automatically, and check out as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch is the one exception. cmd.exe's parser can mis-handle labels and
# goto targets with bare LF, so these stay CRLF on purpose.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Load-bearing: the Workflow tool rejects any script containing a control
# character, and CR is one. Stated explicitly so the intent survives an edit to
# the wildcard above.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or the shebang line breaks with a trailing CR.
*.sh text eol=lf
